caption: The World Ended

In the year 2135, the world ended. It was the third or fourth world war, depending on how you count. Like most wars, it started over a minor incident but escalated due to existing tensions. These were numerous; fissables were still a big factor in energy production, and so they were desired. Rare earth metals, used in creating batteries and magnets, both essential to modern technology and weaponry, were sought after. The developing nations of the early 21st century started moving to fission, fusion, and renewables, and the countries that had previously been subsisting on oil exports suddenly became desperate to seize valuable land. One of the other major points of contention, and possibly the spark that ignited the war, was control over orbital space. It turns out that several major nations had put military hardware in orbit, which they had agreed not to do.

What really doomed the world, however, was almost entirely unexpected: several secret occult societies were performing rituals on the first day of the war, and the blood sacrifices of the dying caused them to succeed, changing the face of the world forever. Every major population center, any metropolitan area with a population over about 6 million, exploded in magical energy, annihilating 4 billion lives in an instant, over one seventh of the world’s population at the time. Magical gates appeared everywhere, in the sky and on the ground. Energy flooded in from these other worlds, sometimes magical, but much of the time just in the form of heat, light, and wind. Storms raged, tsunamis struck, the earth quaked, and war raged on.

Monsters from other worlds invaded. Entire armies of aliens, sometimes wielding stone weapons, sometimes in power armor and wielding weapons that put pre-war man to shame. The first fifty years were defined by death, suffering, and loss. Humans died from starvation and plague more than to monsters, but there were plenty of monsters too. After a time, we adapted, and humanity began to recover slowly.
